WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.454
A lot of meetings are really presentations with a calendar invite.

2
00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:08.646
Here's when a video saves everyone time, and when to keep the meeting.

3
00:00:09.146 --> 00:00:12.974
If the content is mostly one-way, a video often wins.

4
00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:19.222
It reaches the manager, finance and IT, and lands in their morning, whether that's Perth or London.

5
00:00:19.722 --> 00:00:22.609
But keep the meeting for real conversation.

6
00:00:22.659 --> 00:00:26.649
A good rule: if it's mostly you talking, send a video.

7
00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:29.031
If it's mostly them talking, meet.

8
00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:32.511
A video only saves time if people watch it.

9
00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:37.772
Keep it short, tell them why it matters up front, and end with one clear next step.

10
00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:43.783
Instead of waiting a week for a follow-up meeting, send a five-minute narrated deck the next morning.

11
00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:47.315
The next meeting becomes questions, not a presentation.

12
00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:50.837
Put what you'd say in the speaker notes.

13
00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:56.235
On Pro, a.i. directs the video, so your proposal lands like a produced pitch.

14
00:00:56.285 --> 00:00:58.907
Try it at powerpoint2vid.com.

